.TH UIRCDPASSWD 1 "2026-11-19" "uircd 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uircdpasswd \- generate a password entry for miniircd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uircdpasswd
.I name flags
.RI [ password ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uircdpasswd
produces one line for the
.BR miniircd (8)
password file, hashing a password with scrypt. It writes the line to standard
output; redirect or append it to the file named by the server's
.B \-A
option (or the
.RB ' "[auth] passwd" '
setting).
.PP
If
.I password
is omitted it is read from the terminal with echo disabled. The program wipes
the plaintext password and the derived hash from memory before it exits.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I name
The account name, or
.RB ' * '
for a shared entry that any
.RB ' "PASS password" '
matches.
.TP
.I flags
One or more of:
.RB ' c '
(may connect) and
.RB ' o '
(may use
.BR OPER ).
.TP
.I password
The password. Read from the terminal if not given on the command line. Passing
it as an argument may expose it in the process list and shell history; prefer
the prompt.
.SH OUTPUT
The emitted line has the form:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name:scrypt:N:r:p:salt_b64:hash_b64:flags
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.IR N ", " r ", and " p
are the scrypt work parameters, and the salt and hash are Base64. The salt is
drawn from the operating system random source.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, and nonzero on a usage error or if the password could not
be read or hashed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a login account, entering the password at the prompt:
.PP
.RS
.nf
uircdpasswd alice c >> users.passwd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create an operator who may also connect:
.PP
.RS
.nf
uircdpasswd admin co >> users.passwd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a shared connect password:
.PP
.RS
.nf
uircdpasswd '*' c >> users.passwd
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Keep the password file readable only by the account that runs the server; it
holds scrypt hashes. See
.IR SECURITY.md .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR miniircd (8)
